INT. STRANDED GONDOLA CAR, ALPINE CABLEWAY - NIGHT

A glass box swaying slightly in the dark, forty meters above a gorge. Frost on the windows. A single battery lamp. The far lights of a village glitter like spilled sugar.

Five people are packed inside, and a voice on a radio makes six. Skis, packs, a child's sled strapped to the wall. Their breath fogs the glass.

A WOMAN in her fifties sits upright, coat buttoned to the chin, a folded map in her lap. A MAN in his thirties crouches by the door with a phone held high. A TEENAGE GIRL hugs her knees on the bench. An ELDERLY MAN with a cane and a flat cap sits staring at the ceiling. A YOUNG WOMAN in ski patrol red leans against the control panel.

The man's phone shows no signal. He lowers it.

The radio on the control panel crackles.

OPERATOR (V.O.)
(radio, faint)
Gondola Nine, Base. Do you read?

The patrol woman grabs the handset.

PATROL
Base, Gondola Nine. We read. Six aboard, all stable.

OPERATOR (V.O.)
(radio)
Copy six. Power's out on the whole line. Rescue crew's three hours out.

The elderly man grunts. The woman refolds her map, creasing it in half, then in quarters.

PATROL
(to the car)
Three hours. Everyone stay put. Please.

She sets the handset down. The teenager shifts.

TEENAGER
I'm cold. Ines, what do we do?

INES
We sort it.

The man in the door unzips his jacket. He crosses to the bench and spreads it over the teenager. He sits back on his heels. The old man watches him.

ELDERLY MAN
You know her?

MAN
No.

ELDERLY MAN
Hm.

The man looks at the teenager. She shakes her head, once. He turns away.

The gondola lurches. A creak travels the cable from far above. Everyone freezes. The lamp flickers. The glass trembles in its frame.

INES
It's the wind. Just the wind.

WOMAN
It's not the wind.

INES
Ma'am...

WOMAN
It's the cable. Listen.

They listen. A thin, rhythmic singing runs along the steel, rising in pitch.

INES
(into radio, sharp)
Base, Base. We have cable noise. Repeat, cable noise.

OPERATOR (V.O.)
(radio)
Say again, Nine?

INES
Cable noise! Singing, pitching up!

OPERATOR (V.O.)
(radio)
Hold on. Hold on, Nine.

The old man lifts his cane and lays it across his knees like a rifle. The teenager pulls the jacket closer. The man by the door stands, swaying, and puts a hand on the glass. Far below, the gorge exhales cold air.

The singing stops. A long, absolute silence. Then the car drops six inches, stops, and swings.

Nobody screams. The young woman in red just closes her eyes.

WOMAN
(very quiet)
All right. All right.

She takes the teenager's hand. The teenager takes the old man's. He takes the man's. A chain. A shaky one.

INES
(whispering to herself)
Come on, rescue. Come on.

The radio hisses. No voice. The lamp holds, steady and small, as the car sways out over nothing.
